WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.210
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:11.209
Here's a really cool computer animation problem,
you'll love this one: we take a digital character,

00:00:11.209 --> 00:00:17.599
specify an initial pose and a target objective
- for instance, a position somewhere in space.

00:00:17.599 --> 00:00:23.060
And the algorithm has to come up with a series
of smooth movements and contact interactions

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.439
to obtain this goal.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:29.339
But these movements have to be physically
meaningful, such as that self-intersection

00:00:29.339 --> 00:00:33.590
and non-natural contortions have to be avoided
throughout this process.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.710
Keep an eye out for the white cross to see
where the target positions are.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:43.420
Now, for starters, it's cool to see that humanoids
are handled quite well, but here comes the

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.830
super fun part: the mathematical formulation
of this optimization problem does not depend

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:55.570
on the body type at all, therefore, both humanoids
and almost arbitrarily crazy, non-humanoid

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.030
creatures are also supported.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:02.600
If we make this problem a bit more interesting,
and make changes to the terrain to torment

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:07.550
these creatures, we'll notice that they come
up with sensible movements to overcome these

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:08.870
challenges.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.610
These results are absolutely amazing.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:17.180
And this includes obtaining highly non-trivial
target poses, such as handstands.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.860
The goal does not necessarily have to be a
position, but it can be an orientation or

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:30.030
a given pose as well.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:35.240
We can even add multiple characters to the
environment and ask them to join forces to

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.260
accomplish a task together.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:41.800
And here you can see that both characters
take into consideration the actions of the

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.870
other one and not only compensate accordingly,
but they make sure that this happens in a

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:50.000
way that brings them closer to their objective.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.799
It is truly incredible to see how these digital
characters can learn such complex animations

00:01:55.799 --> 00:01:57.540
in a matter of minutes.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.390
A true testament to the power of mathematical
optimization algorithms.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:07.260
If you wish to hear more about how optimization
works, we've had a previous episode on this

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:12.719
topic, make sure to check it out, it includes
a rigorous mathematical study on how to make

00:02:12.719 --> 00:02:14.510
the perfect vegetable stew.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.459
The link is available in the video description.

00:02:16.459 --> 00:02:21.129
And, if you feel a bit addicted to Two Minute
Papers, please note that these episodes are

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:26.400
available in early access through Patreon,
click on the icon with the "p" at the ending

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.959
screen if you're interested.

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:32.069
It also helps us a great deal in improving
the quality of the series.

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:36.890
We try to be as transparent as possible, and
every now and then we write a technical memo

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:42.389
to summarize the recent improvements we were
able to make, and this is all thanks to you.

00:02:42.389 --> 00:02:46.489
If you're interested, I've put a link to the
latest post in the video description.

00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:50.409
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

